# Laguna Racer sound circuits -- netlist converted from MAME
#
# Colin Douglas Howell's netlists for Midway's 8080 driving and shooting games are the most
# carefully documented in MAME: every value is argued from the schematic, the PCB drawings
# and board photos, and the file says where they disagree. ICs are named by their board
# position. He declares each LM3900 amplifier on its own, numbered _1 to _4 as the LM3900
# data sheet numbers them; they are written on their package's pins here. The CPU's sound
# latches arrive as ports, named after the latch pin MAME's LOGIC_INPUT stands in for
# (I_F4_2 is latch F4 pin 2).
#
# The noise generator is the real circuit (FAST_HLE_NOISE_GEN off): a 1N5239 zener
# biased near breakdown through RNOISE0 from +12V, amplified by the LM3900 at H4.
# MAME adds its own noise source in series with the zener, which is how it models the
# zener's noise; here the zener's anode and H4's input are one node. The schematics do
# not number the discrete parts; Howell gives them the Super Speed Race numbers, which
# mostly correspond, and invents RNOISE0-3, CNOISE0 and CNOISE for the noise
# generator's, which Super Speed Race does not have.
#
# Source: MAME mame0289 (commit f34f02505e32), src/mame/midw8080/nl_280zzzap.cpp,
# NETLIST_START(lagunar). Licence CC0-1.0 by the file's own header (line 1),
# copyright-holders Colin Douglas Howell (line 2).
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mamedev/mame/mame0289/src/mame/midw8080/nl_280zzzap.cpp
# Converted by tools/mame_netlist.py; checked by tools/build_board_nets.py.
#
# What the conversion means. MAME's netlists are written to be simulated, so this is a
# second reading of the sheet by someone else, not a read of it: designators and pin
# numbers are MAME's unless the notes say the sheet was read to correct them. Names on
# `label` lines are MAME's, which mostly follow the sheet's with Q for an overbar; `net`
# is used only for the supply rails. A `port` is a net that leaves what this file
# describes -- a signal from the CPU or the other board, a cabinet control, or a source
# MAME simulates with a stand-in rather than the circuit.
#
#   net NAME: PIN ...     a supply rail
#   label NAME: PIN ...   a net MAME names
#   wire PIN PIN ...      an unnamed connection
#   port NAME: PIN ...    a net that leaves this file (see above)
#   nc PIN ...            a pin with nothing attached
#   parallel PIN ...      outputs the board sums on one node by design
#   value REF VALUE       a passive's value, in the unit MAME's own macro uses
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. Diode pin 1 is the cathode; transistors keep B, C and E;
# MAME does not record which way round an electrolytic goes, so C pins are MAME's 1/2.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Undone: what MAME does to simulate the board that the board does not do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# line 71: #define CONVERGENCE_FRONTIERS 1 -> 0 (solver partitioning, with resistor values adjusted to suit)
# line 79: #define FAST_HLE_NOISE_GEN 1 -> 0 (high-level stand-in for a real circuit)
# line 86: #define REMOVE_POST_CRASH_NOISE_GLITCHES 1 -> 0 (a voltage limiter MAME adds on an op-amp output)
# line 1040: NET_C(GND, G5.10, G5.11, G5.12, J4.6, J4.8, J4.9, G4.10, G4.11, G4.12) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Switches G5_D, J4_C, and G4_D are unused." block
# line 582: SYS_NOISE_MT_N(NOISE) is MAME's noise source in series with the zener it models the noise of; its two ends are one node here
# ZD_1N5239 written 1N5239, where MAME gives its model (D(BV=9.1 IBV=0.020 NBV=1)) -- a 9.1V zener; MAME's name says which
# MAME declares one section of these packages at a time; each is written on its package's
# pins by the data sheet's numbering of the sections (SECTION_PINS in the converter): UH4 LM3900 (H4_1, H4_2, H4_3, H4_4); UH5 LM3900 (H5_1, H5_2, H5_3, H5_4); UJ3 LM3900 (J3_1, J3_2, J3_3, J3_4); UJ5 LM3900 (J5_1, J5_2, J5_3, J5_4)
# Stand-ins that are not parts (the nets they drive are ports): CLOCK(NCLK), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F4_2), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F4_5), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F4_7), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F4_10), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F5_2), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F5_5), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F5_7), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F5_10), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F5_12), LOGIC_INPUT(I_F5_15)
#
# Compared with both Laguna Racer game logic sheets, A084-90700-B622 (doc 1461b5f1ef33) and
# -A622 (doc 2e04e6b435f0), on 2026-10-20 -- every wire and port line of this file, not a
# sample. The two sheets have one circuit and one set of pin numbers; 86 of the 89 lines
# agree on both, and all sixteen LM3900 amplifiers are on the pins the data sheet numbers
# them by, as this file puts them. The noise generator agrees part for part (the zener is
# printed IN5239). Two pins are not numbered on either sheet and are MAME's: G4 switch A's
# input (pin 1 by elimination) and the MC3340's ground and output (3 and 7).
#
# The three that differ are one switch. Both sheets print the bottom (76 Hz) oscillator's J4
# switch as pins 4, 3 and 5 -- switch B, which the middle oscillator also uses -- and MAME
# puts it on 10, 11 and 12 (switch D). Its comment: 'mislabeled pin numbers on the
# schematics for 280-ZZZAP and both Laguna Racer versions ... The Super Speed Race schematic
# correctly shows this oscillator using pins 10, 11, 12'. Two oscillators cannot share one
# switch, so this file follows MAME; it is a correction of the sheet, logged, not read.
#
# Neither sheet draws the LM3900s' or CD4016s' supply pins, so +5V on 14 and ground on 7 are
# MAME's ('these aren't shown on the schematics'); nor the unused switches G5_D, J4_C and
# G4_D, so whether the board ties their pins or leaves them open is not on the sheet. The
# sheet's +12V here is '+12V DECOUPLED' -- connector A pin 3 through 100 ohms with 500uF to
# ground, neither of which MAME has -- and the LM377 bridge amplifier at J1 that OUTPUT and
# J5 pin 9 feed is on the sheet and not in MAME.
#
# This is the Laguna Racer variant, and every value in it agrees with the B622 sheet,
# including the six where B622 and A622 differ (R46 2.2M, R48 470K, R51 470K, R11 120K, C18
# 22uF tantalum, R30 100K -- the last read with moderate confidence, its middle digit faint).

# part UG4 CD4016
# part UG5 CD4016
# part UH2 MC3340  (MAME: MC3340_H2)
# part UH4 LM3900
# part UH5 LM3900
# part UJ3 LM3900
# part UJ4 CD4016
# part UJ5 LM3900

value R1 330K
value R2 10K
value R3 820K
value R4 270K
value R5 39K
value R6 62
value R7 82K
value R8 39K
value R9 39K
